WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:01.830
- [Instructor] What do you think it means

00:00:01.830 --> 00:00:03.750
to be a good citizen?

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:06.150
What does a good citizen do?

00:00:06.150 --> 00:00:10.170
Take a minute to you imagine
your idea of a good citizen.

00:00:10.170 --> 00:00:12.770
What characteristics
does that person have?

00:00:12.770 --> 00:00:15.320
What actions does that person take

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.220
that contribute to their status

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:20.240
as an active and virtuous citizen?

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.360
I encourage you to pause the video here

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:25.573
and write down a few words or examples.

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:29.270
Okay, so what did you come up with?

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:33.030
Did you think of someone
who obeys the law,

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:35.460
treats others with kindness,

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:40.090
volunteers, maybe spends
their money wisely?

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:43.510
What about someone who
is active in politics,

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:47.190
or organizes community
responses to problems

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.490
like starting a food drive?

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:51.280
Or maybe you thought about someone

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:54.340
who tries to solve problems facing society

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:56.700
by advocating for change,

00:00:56.700 --> 00:01:00.640
maybe working to change laws
or larger social structures

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:03.730
in order to promote justice and equality.

00:01:03.730 --> 00:01:05.550
So which one is right?

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:06.710
Okay, yeah, you guessed it.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:07.910
It's a trick question.

00:01:07.910 --> 00:01:11.410
In fact, these are all
models of good citizenship,

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:15.010
and I wanna to dive a little
more deeply into each of them

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:16.940
to give you a sense of what they mean,

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.690
because if you compare
what you jotted down

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.440
when I asked you to
imagine a good citizen,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.940
to what your friend or your
parent or your teacher imagined,

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.770
you might find that they had something

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:29.720
entirely different in mind.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:33.210
So it's helpful to be able
to name different visions

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:34.630
for good citizenship,

00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:37.430
so that you can probe a little more deeply

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:39.140
into what someone really means

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.080
when they're talking about it.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.890
The first model you might see

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:46.840
is that of the
personally-responsible citizen.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:50.530
This is a model of citizenship
that focuses on the actions

00:01:50.530 --> 00:01:52.280
an individual can take

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:55.230
to be a good member of their community.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.650
A personally-responsible citizen

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:01.800
fulfills their personal
obligations and acts responsibly.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.060
They pay their taxes, vote, obey the law,

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.510
and volunteer to help the less fortunate.

00:02:08.510 --> 00:02:10.980
The are honest and work hard.

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:13.450
What separates this
model from the other two

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:14.630
we're going to talk about

00:02:14.630 --> 00:02:17.100
is that the personally-responsible citizen

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:20.390
generally doesn't try to change society

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:23.640
or lead efforts to
improve their community.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:25.690
They just do their part.

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:27.330
The second model I wanna talk about

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.930
is participatory citizenship.

00:02:29.930 --> 00:02:34.690
This model emphasizes community
action and organizing.

00:02:34.690 --> 00:02:37.760
The participatory citizen
might create a group

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:40.480
to advocate for environmental protection,

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:44.620
or organize a blood drive at
their school or workplace.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:47.260
They believe in working
collectively with others

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:49.890
to address problems in their communities.

00:02:49.890 --> 00:02:53.640
They tend to work through
established systems and groups

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:55.210
to enact change,

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:57.820
which distinguishes
them from the last model

00:02:57.820 --> 00:03:01.500
I wanna talk about, the
justice-oriented citizen.

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.860
A big difference between
the justice-oriented citizen

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.820
and the participatory citizen

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:10.360
is that participatory citizens
don't attempt to change

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.730
big underlying social problems
like poverty or racism.

00:03:14.730 --> 00:03:19.170
They organize to meet the
challenges of the world as it is.

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:22.630
So let's talk a little bit
more about this third model,

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:25.130
the justice-oriented citizen.

00:03:25.130 --> 00:03:28.140
The justice-oriented citizen is interested

00:03:28.140 --> 00:03:32.510
in addressing social issues
and the larger structures

00:03:32.510 --> 00:03:34.260
that lead to injustice.

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.650
They try to take a step
back from society as it is

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.730
to ask why problems happen,

00:03:39.730 --> 00:03:43.250
and seek solutions to their root causes.

00:03:43.250 --> 00:03:45.960
So for a justice-oriented citizen,

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:48.710
giving to charity or organizing a group

00:03:48.710 --> 00:03:51.600
to combat a social problem isn't enough.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.820
They wanna stop that problem
from happening in the future.

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:57.870
Now, I'm not telling you this
because I want to convince you

00:03:57.870 --> 00:04:02.260
that there's a correct or a
best way to be a good citizen.

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:03.910
All three of these models

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.000
are doing good in their communities,

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.100
just in different ways and
with different areas of focus.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.490
I'm also not saying
that you have to choose

00:04:12.490 --> 00:04:14.600
one of these models to follow.

00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:17.030
There's nothing stopping
you from doing a little bit

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:20.050
of all three, or taking
one approach with an issue

00:04:20.050 --> 00:04:23.780
you care about, and a different
approach with another issue,

00:04:23.780 --> 00:04:27.970
but understanding these different
approaches to citizenship

00:04:27.970 --> 00:04:31.120
will help you communicate
more clearly with others

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:35.290
about how you can work together
to improve your community.

00:04:35.290 --> 00:04:38.170
Before we go, let's do a quick scenario

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:40.510
so you can get in some practice.

00:04:40.510 --> 00:04:42.820
I'll give you some examples of students

00:04:42.820 --> 00:04:46.100
responding to a problem at their school,

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:48.680
And you see if you can identify

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:52.900
which model of citizenship
each student represents.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:54.140
So here's the problem.

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.930
The marching band
doesn't have enough money

00:04:56.930 --> 00:05:00.050
to attend the "The
Battle of the Big Bands,"

00:05:00.050 --> 00:05:01.280
where they're going to compete

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:03.960
for best regional marching band.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:08.420
Gabe, who plays the tuba,
decides to organize a bake sale

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:11.060
in order to raise money for the band trip.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:12.820
Bodie, who plays the flute,

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:15.780
bakes brownies and buys rice crispy treats

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:18.690
for all of her friends
to support the cause.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:21.200
Nadia, who plays alto sax

00:05:21.200 --> 00:05:24.050
wonders why the marching band
doesn't have as much funding

00:05:24.050 --> 00:05:26.110
as some other school clubs.

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:28.390
She presents in front of the school board

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:30.560
requesting equal funding per student

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.900
for extra curricular activities.

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:35.930
Which model of citizenship

00:05:35.930 --> 00:05:38.890
does each of these students represent.

00:05:38.890 --> 00:05:42.580
Gabe is modeling
participatory citizenship.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:46.890
He is organizing a community
response to a problem.

00:05:46.890 --> 00:05:50.970
Bodie is modeling
personally-responsible citizenship.

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:53.440
She does her part in the bake sale.

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:57.100
Nadia is modeling
justice-oriented citizenship.

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:59.850
She wants to make sure that
the systems are in place

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:01.980
to keep problems like this from happening

00:06:01.980 --> 00:06:04.000
in the first place.

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:06.910
So which model of citizenship do you think

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:09.400
you most often practice?

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:13.090
Does your family, school, social group,

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:15.870
promote one of these
models more than another?

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:18.470
Do you think there are
other models of citizenship

00:06:18.470 --> 00:06:20.433
that we haven't identified here?

